] as const;
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="How Emploist handles personal data: what we collect, how AI ranking uses job applications, who we share data with, where it's stored, and your rights."
  updated="October 25, 2026"
  sections={sections}
>
  <Fragment slot="summary">
    <ul>
      <li>
        Emploist is operated by Navaneeth CT, an individual based in India. We're responsible for your Emploist account
        data, such as your name, email address, and sign-in records.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you applied for a job on a careers page, the employer is in charge of your application, and we handle it for
        them. Read the employer's privacy notice, and contact them first with questions or
        requests. <a href="#applicants">More for job applicants</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        We collect what we need to run Emploist: account and company details, sign-in and security records such as IP
        addresses, and limited billing details. Dodo Payments, which sells our plan, handles payment details. We never
        receive your full card number.
      </li>
      <li>
        When an employer has AI ranking on, the text of each resume and the answers to the employer's questions
        (except phone numbers) are sent to our AI provider, TypeSafe, in the US, to score how well the application
        matches the job. The match score never rejects, moves, or emails anyone by itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        We don't sell personal data or use it for advertising. We use no analytics and no tracking cookies.
      </li>
      <li>
        Our server and database are in Singapore, and our file storage is in the Asia-Pacific region. We access data from
        India, and some providers process it in the US and Japan. <a href="#international-transfers"
          >International transfers</a
        > explains how we protect it.
      </li>
      <li>
        You can ask to access, correct, or delete your personal data, or object to how we use it, among other rights.
        Email us at <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>This is only a summary. The full policy below is what applies.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="who-we-are">1. Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    Emploist is an applicant tracking system for businesses, with hosted careers pages and optional AI ranking of
    applications. Emploist is operated by Navaneeth CT, an individual based in India ("Emploist", "we", "us").
  </p>
  <p>
    This policy explains how we handle personal data when you use the Emploist app at app.emploist.com, visit
    emploist.com, apply for a job on a careers page that runs on Emploist, or contact us.
  </p>
  <p>
    We take questions and requests by email only, at <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a>.
    Our privacy contact is Navaneeth CT, at the same address.
  </p>

  <h3>Grievance Officer (India)</h3>
  <p>
    Under India's Information Technology Act and its rules, our Grievance Officer is Navaneeth CT, reachable
    at <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a>. We'll acknowledge a grievance within 24 hours and
    resolve it within 15 days of receiving it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="scope">2. What this policy covers</h2>
  <p>Data protection laws give different responsibilities to different organizations:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A <strong>controller</strong> decides why and how personal data is used, and is responsible for it.</li>
    <li>A <strong>processor</strong> handles personal data on a controller's behalf and follows its instructions.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Some laws use other words for the same idea. For example, California law says "business" and "service provider",
    and India's law says "data fiduciary" and "data processor".
  </p>
  <p>
    Companies that use Emploist ("customers", called "employers" in the section for job applicants) put their own
    content into it, such as jobs, careers page content and code, application forms, emails, and comments. Applicants
    send them applications through it, with resumes and answers. Together with the match scores and other records
    Emploist creates from them, we call this "Customer Data", as our <a href="/terms/">Terms</a> define it. Each customer
    is responsible for its Customer Data, usually as its controller (a recruiter may act for a client instead), and we
    process it on the customer's behalf.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>If you are</th>
        <th>Our role</th>
        <th>What applies</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>A team member using the Emploist app for your company, as an admin or a member</td>
        <td>
          Controller of your account data, such as your name, email address, sign-in records, and billing contact
          details. Processor for the work you do for your company, such as your comments, which is part of its Customer
          Data.
        </td>
        <td>
          This policy. For your company's Customer Data, also our <a href="/terms/#data-processing">data processing
          terms</a> with your company.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>A visitor to emploist.com</td>
        <td>Controller</td>
        <td>This policy</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>A job applicant who applied through a careers page that runs on Emploist</td>
        <td>Processor. The employer is the controller of your application.</td>
        <td>
          The employer's privacy notice. Our <a href="/terms/#data-processing">data processing terms</a> govern how we
          handle your application for the employer. <a href="#applicants">For job applicants</a> explains how Emploist
          works.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Someone named in a customer's content, such as a contact in a job description</td>
        <td>Processor for the customer</td>
        <td>The customer's privacy notice and our <a href="/terms/#data-processing">data processing terms</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Someone invited to join a company's team on Emploist</td>
        <td>Controller of the invitation we send and our record of it. The company decides whom to invite.</td>
        <td>This policy</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Whatever your role, we're the controller of the technical data our servers record to keep Emploist working and
    secure, such as IP addresses in request logs. This policy covers that data too.
  </p>

  <h2 id="applicants">3. For job applicants</h2>
  <p>
    This section is for you if you applied for a job through a careers page that runs on Emploist. These pages are at
    addresses like company.emploist.com, or on the employer's own domain.
  </p>

  <h3>The employer is in charge of your application</h3>
  <p>
    The employer whose careers page you applied on decides what the form asks, how your application is used, who on
    their team has access to Emploist, whether AI ranking is used, and how long your application is kept. That makes the
    employer the controller of your application. Emploist provides the software and handles your application on the
    employer's behalf. In this section, "employer" means the organization whose careers page you applied on. If that's a
    recruiter hiring for a client, the client may also be responsible for your application.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the employer's privacy notice is the one that applies to your application. Please read it, and contact the
    employer first with any questions or requests about your data. The rest of this section explains how Emploist
    itself handles your application.
  </p>

  <h3>What a careers page collects</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Your full name and email address.</li>
    <li>Your resume or CV, as a PDF or Word (DOCX) file.</li>
    <li>
      Your answers to the employer's questions. The employer chooses the questions, which can include your phone number.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Emploist also keeps the date you applied, the hiring stage your application is in, and its history. Everyone on the
    employer's team in Emploist can see your application. They can comment on it, move it between stages, export it,
    and delete it.
  </p>

  <h3>Your IP address and browser details</h3>
  <p>
    Your application's record doesn't include your IP address, browser details, or any cookie or device information.
    We use your IP address briefly, without saving it, to limit how many applications come from one address, which
    helps keep out spam. But like any website, our servers keep request logs. They record your IP address and browser
    details when you visit a careers page or apply, and the log entry for your application includes its ID, so the two
    can be linked. We're the controller of these logs.
  </p>

  <h3>Emails about your application</h3>
  <p>
    The employer can set up emails that go out when you apply or when your application moves to a new stage, such as an
    interview invitation or a rejection. The employer chooses which of these emails to send and can change what they
    say. We send them through our email provider,
    Resend, from Emploist's sending address, in the employer's name. If the employer has set a reply-to address, your
    replies go to the employer. If the employer hasn't set a reply-to address, replies can't be delivered, and we don't
    receive them, so contact the employer directly.
  </p>

  <h3>AI ranking</h3>
  <p>
    Employers can have Emploist rank applications with AI. It's on by default for new companies, and while the company's Plan is
    active, the employer's admins can turn it off or on for the whole company. Our Terms require employers that use it to tell you
    about it. While it's on, Emploist's default careers page shows a notice that AI helps review applications.
  </p>
  <p>When AI ranking is on, we send our AI provider, TypeSafe AI, Inc., in the United States:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      the text of your resume (for a very long resume, the first 40,000 characters, and for a PDF, the first 20 pages)
    </li>
    <li>your answers to the employer's questions, except phone numbers</li>
    <li>the job's title, department, employment type, and description</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We don't send your name and email fields, or the job's location, as separate items. But your resume's text is sent
    as it is, so whatever it contains, such as your name, contact details, and work history, is sent too.
  </p>
  <p>
    TypeSafe's AI scores how well your application matches the job on four things: skills, experience, seniority fit,
    and qualifications. Emploist combines these into a match score, shown as a percentage. Skills and experience count
    most, then seniority fit, then qualifications. If the job doesn't ask for any qualifications, that part is left out.
    Your answers can adjust the scores slightly.
  </p>
  <p>
    The match score is meant to help the employer decide whose applications to review first. The employer's team sees it
    with your application, and their list of applications is sorted by match score unless they choose another order. The
    match score never rejects you, moves your application to another stage, or sends you an email by itself. Only people
    on the employer's team move applications. Like any AI, it can be wrong, so our Terms require employers not to rely on
    the score alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    While AI ranking is off, new applications aren't sent to TypeSafe. If the employer turns it back on, its admins can
    choose to rank the applications that arrived in the meantime.
  </p>
  <p>
    Emploist keeps the match score and its breakdown with your application. We don't keep the text taken from your
    resume. It's read from your file each time the application is ranked, and discarded afterwards. How TypeSafe handles
    the data we send is described in <a href="#subprocessors">Sharing and subprocessors</a>, and how it's protected
    in <a href="#international-transfers">International transfers</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Your choices and rights</h3>
  <p>Contact the employer if you want to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>object to AI ranking of your application, or ask for it to be reviewed by a person without AI</li>
    <li>ask how your application was assessed</li>
    <li>access, correct, or delete your data, or use any other right you have</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The employer makes these decisions. In Emploist, the employer's team can export a job's applications and delete
    applications or whole jobs, and its admins can delete the company. Admins can turn AI ranking off for the whole
    company, but Emploist can't turn it off for a single application. Some requests need our help, which we give when
    the employer asks by email: correcting the details in an application, which Emploist can't edit, providing match
    scores, their breakdown, and comments, which aren't in the export, and deleting applications or jobs while the
    employer's company is read-only.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you contact us about your application instead, we'll forward your request to the employer and let you know we've
    done so. We can't decide on the employer's behalf. For the request logs and other data we control ourselves, you can
    contact us directly. See <a href="#your-rights">Your rights</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>How long your application is kept</h3>
  <p>
    The employer decides. Your application stays in Emploist until the employer deletes it, deletes the job, or deletes
    its company. Under our <a href="/terms/#suspension-and-termination">Terms</a>, if a company has had no active Plan
    for 12 months in a row, we may delete it and all its data. We'll email its admins at least 30 days before we do.
  </p>
  <p>
    A few things can outlast your application, such as a notification about it that someone on the employer's team has
    already read, which keeps your name and the job title, copies in our backups and logs, and data already sent to
    TypeSafe or Resend. <a href="#retention">How long we keep information</a> explains these exceptions.
  </p>

  <h3>Careers pages and other websites</h3>
  <p>
    Emploist sets no cookies on careers pages, and scripts don't run on them. But an employer can design its own careers
    page and email design, and these can load images, fonts, or styles from other websites. When your browser or email
    app loads them, those websites may receive your IP address and browser details, and may set their own cookies. They
    handle this data under their own policies.
  </p>

  <h2 id="information-we-collect">4. Information we collect</h2>
  <p>
    This section covers the personal data we handle as controller, and what's part of your company's Customer Data. For
    applicants' data, see <a href="#applicants">For job applicants</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Account information</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Name and email address.</strong> If you sign in with Google or Microsoft, we get them from your Google or
      Microsoft account. If you sign in with a code sent to your email, you type them in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sign-in codes.</strong> When you sign in with an emailed code, we store a scrambled (hashed) copy of the
      code with your email address. Codes expire after 5 minutes.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>You need to give us your name and email address to use Emploist. Without them, we can't create your account.</p>

  <h3 id="google-microsoft">Google and Microsoft sign-in</h3>
  <p>If you sign in with Google or Microsoft, this is how we handle the data they give us:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>What we get.</strong> Your name, email address, whether your email address is verified, account ID, a
      link to your profile picture if they send one, and sign-in tokens. Microsoft sign-in also asks for permission to
      read your basic profile and to keep that access over time, so Microsoft also gives us a long-lived token (a
      "refresh token"). We don't ask for access to your email, files, calendar, contacts, or anything else in your
      account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>How we use it.</strong> Only to sign you in, recognize you, and show your name and email address in
      Emploist, for example to your teammates and to people you invite. We don't use the tokens to access your Google
      or Microsoft account or any data in it, and we don't show your profile picture.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>How we store it.</strong> With your Emploist account, in Singapore, protected
      as <a href="#security">Security</a> describes, until you delete your account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Who we share it with.</strong> Only as <a href="#subprocessors">Sharing and subprocessors</a> describes.
      We don't share the tokens, sell this data, or use it for advertising.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your controls.</strong> You can remove Emploist's access in your Google Account or Microsoft account
      settings. For a work or school Microsoft account, your organization's administrator may manage this. Removing
      access doesn't delete your Emploist account or the tokens we've stored. Deleting your Emploist account does. You
      can also ask us for a copy of this data, as <a href="#your-rights">Your rights</a> explains.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What you create for your company</h3>
  <p>
    When you set up a company, you add its name and careers page address and, if you like, a logo, a description, and a
    brand color. Admins can also add a reply-to address for emails to applicants and connect a custom domain. Your logo
    is public, on your careers page and in emails to applicants.
  </p>
  <p>
    The work you do in Emploist for your company, such as the jobs you create, the comments you write and the people you
    mention, and the applications you move, is stored with your name. Your teammates can see your name, your email
    address, and this activity. All of this is part of your company's Customer Data. Your company controls it, and we
    handle it on your company's behalf, so it stays with your company if you leave or delete your account.
  </p>

  <h3>Invitations</h3>
  <p>
    When an admin invites someone, we store the invited person's email address, their role, who invited them, and the
    invitation's status. We email them a link that expires after 7 days, and we store only a scrambled (hashed) version
    of the link's secret part. The email and the page it opens show the name of the person who sent the invitation, or
    their email address if they haven't set a name.
  </p>

  <h3>Billing information</h3>
  <p>
    Dodo Payments is the reseller and merchant of record for our plan and credit packs. Dodo sells them to customers,
    takes payment, handles sales tax, and issues invoices.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      When an admin first checks out, we send Dodo that admin's email address and the company's name. We don't update
      that email later, for example if that admin leaves the company.
    </li>
    <li>
      You enter payment details on Dodo's own pages, and we never receive your full card number. Dodo's messages to us
      about payments can include billing details, such as the billing address and the last four digits of the card, but
      we don't store them.
    </li>
    <li>
      From Dodo, we receive and store your company's Dodo customer ID, your plan's status and dates, and payment IDs that
      we use to add AI credits. We also keep records of your AI credits and how they're used.
    </li>
    <li>
      To keep free trials to one per company and per person, we keep a record of the admins of a deleted company that
      used its free trial.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Security and technical information</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Sessions.</strong> When you sign in, we create a session with a secret session token, which your browser
      keeps in a cookie. We record the session with your IP address and your browser's user agent, which describes your
      browser and device. For IPv6 addresses, we store only the network part, not the full address.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Request logs.</strong> Our servers log each request to the app and to careers pages. A log entry includes
      the IP address, browser details, the page the request came from (if the browser sends it), the address of the page
      or data requested, and the response's status and headers. Some of these can include applicants' details. A search for an applicant's
      name in the app puts it in the logged address. When someone on a team opens a resume, its file name, which often
      includes the applicant's name, is logged with the response. And the log entry for each application received links
      the applicant's IP address and browser details to the application's ID. We don't log the contents of
      applications, comments, or uploaded files, and we remove cookies, sign-in secrets, and invitation links from our
      logs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rate limits.</strong> We use IP addresses briefly, in memory, to limit how many requests come from one
      address. These counts aren't saved.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Support</h3>
  <p>
    If you email us, we receive your email address, your message, and anything you include with it. Our email inbox
    provider stores these emails for us.
  </p>

  <h3>Visitors to emploist.com</h3>
  <p>
    Our website has no analytics, cookies, forms, or third-party scripts. The provider that hosts it receives standard
    request data, such as your IP address, your browser details, and the page you asked for, in its server logs.
  </p>

  <h3 id="sensitive-information">Sensitive information</h3>
  <p>
    We don't ask for health, biometric, bank account, or payment card information, and Emploist doesn't ask you to
    create a password. We do keep some secrets that sign people in, which some laws treat as passwords:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>sign-in codes, stored only in hashed form</li>
    <li>session tokens, which keep you signed in</li>
    <li>Google or Microsoft sign-in tokens, stored with your account</li>
    <li>the secret part of invitation links, stored only in hashed form</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We use them only to sign people in. Applicants sometimes include sensitive details in their resumes. The employer is
    responsible for that data.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-we-use-information">5. How we use information</h2>
  <p>
    We use personal data only for the purposes below. If you're in the European Economic Area (EEA) or the UK, the table
    also shows the legal basis we rely on.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Purpose</th>
        <th>Information used</th>
        <th>Legal basis</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Providing Emploist: running your account and company, showing your team's work, running careers pages, and
          sending the emails your company sets up
        </td>
        <td>
          Account information. We also process your company's Customer Data, including your work for it, as
          our <a href="/terms/#data-processing">data processing terms</a> describe.
        </td>
        <td>Legitimate interests: providing the service your company signed up for</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Signing you in and keeping Emploist secure: checking sign-ins, keeping sessions, limiting request rates, and
          preventing and investigating spam and abuse
        </td>
        <td>Account, session, and log information, including IP addresses</td>
        <td>Legitimate interests: keeping Emploist, our users, and applicants safe</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Billing: running free trials and the plan with Dodo, tracking AI credits, and preventing repeat trials</td>
        <td>Billing information and your email address</td>
        <td>
          Legitimate interests: running your company's plan with Dodo, preventing trial abuse, and keeping the tax and
          accounting records required by the laws that apply to us
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Support: answering your questions and requests</td>
        <td>Your emails and any account information we need to help</td>
        <td>Legitimate interests: helping the people who use Emploist</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Service emails: sign-in codes, invitations, and important notices, such as changes to our prices, Terms,
          subprocessors, or this policy, security incidents, and planned deletion of an inactive company
        </td>
        <td>Name and email address</td>
        <td>
          Legitimate interests: signing you in, letting companies invite their team, and keeping customers informed
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Finding and fixing problems and keeping Emploist working, using our logs and support conversations</td>
        <td>
          Log and support information. When these include Customer Data, such as an applicant's name in a search, we use
          it only to fix problems for the customer.
        </td>
        <td>Legitimate interests: keeping Emploist working well</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Running emploist.com</td>
        <td>Standard request data, such as IP addresses</td>
        <td>Legitimate interests: running and securing our website</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Complying with the law, enforcing our Terms, and handling legal claims</td>
        <td>Any relevant information</td>
        <td>
          Legitimate interests: complying with the laws that apply to us, such as Indian law, and protecting our rights.
          Legal obligation, where EU or UK law requires the processing.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    If you're personally a party to our contract, we also rely on that contract for providing Emploist, billing,
    support, and service emails.
  </p>

  <h3 id="right-to-object">Your right to object</h3>
  <p>
    <strong
      >If you're in the EEA or the UK, you can object at any time, for reasons related to your situation, to our use of
      your personal data based on legitimate interests.</strong
    > Most of what this section describes relies on legitimate interests. To object,
    email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a>. We'll then stop, unless we have compelling
    legitimate grounds that override your interests, rights, and freedoms, or we need the data for legal claims. If we
    ever send you marketing emails, you can object to them at any time, and we'll stop.
  </p>

  <h3>What we don't do</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      We don't sell personal data, and we don't "share" it for cross-context behavioral advertising, as California law
      defines those terms.
    </li>
    <li>We don't use personal data for advertising, and we don't use analytics or tracking tools.</li>
    <li>
      We don't use Customer Data, including applications and resumes, to train AI models. TypeSafe's published terms say
      it won't use the data we send to train its AI models without our consent, and we don't give that consent.
    </li>
    <li>
      We use Customer Data only to provide, secure, and support Emploist for the customer, including finding and fixing
      problems, as our <a href="/terms/#data-processing">data processing terms</a> describe. That includes Customer Data
      that appears in our logs or in emails to our support address.
    </li>
    <li>
      We don't make decisions about our users based solely on automated processing that have legal or similarly
